<GuideArticle
  title="Wix vs. a Custom Website: The Real Tradeoffs | Brighter Sites"
  description="Compare the convenience of a website builder with the control and flexibility of a custom-built site without pretending either approach is right for everyone."
  category="Website Foundations"
  heading="Wix vs. a custom website: the real tradeoffs"
  lead="Website builders make it possible for almost anyone to publish a site. A custom-built site offers a different kind of control. The useful question is not which option is universally better—it is which responsibilities you want to own."
>
  <h2>Wix solves a real problem.</h2>
  <p>
    A website builder can bundle hosting, visual editing, templates, forms, and
    other common tools into one managed platform. For a business owner who wants
    to build and manage the site personally, that convenience can be valuable.
  </p>
  <p>
    The platform removes much of the infrastructure work. You do not have to
    make every hosting, deployment, framework, caching, or security decision
    yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Convenience does not remove the website work.</h2>
  <p>
    The builder can provide tools, but the business still has to decide what the
    site should say, which pages are needed, how services should be organized,
    what the mobile experience should feel like, how search tools should be
    connected, and what should change when the data shows a problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    That distinction matters because the visible act of dragging elements onto a
    page is only one part of building a useful website.
  </p>

  <h2>A custom site trades platform convenience for control.</h2>
  <p>
    A custom-built site can be designed around the needs of the business rather
    than around the conventions of a particular visual editor. The developer
    has more control over markup, architecture, code, performance, deployment,
    integrations, and how the project evolves.
  </p>
  <p>
    Brighter Sites may deploy custom static sites through Cloudflare because
    that approach can provide fast global delivery, flexible code ownership, and
    a clean separation between the website and the tools used to manage other
    parts of the business. That is an implementation choice—not an SEO shortcut.
  </p>

  <div class="guide-callout">
    <strong>Custom code does not automatically rank higher.</strong>
    <p>
      Search engines do not reward a site merely because it was hand-coded or
      deployed on Cloudflare. Search performance still depends on relevance,
      content, architecture, technical health, reputation, competition, and
      other signals.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The biggest differences are often control, portability, and responsibility.</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Builder platform:</strong> more of the infrastructure is packaged together, but the site remains tied to the platform's system and limitations.</li>
    <li><strong>Custom site:</strong> more implementation choices are available, but someone has to make and maintain those choices responsibly.</li>
    <li><strong>DIY builder:</strong> the business owner often becomes the designer, content planner, SEO administrator, analytics reviewer, and ongoing maintainer.</li>
    <li><strong>Managed custom site:</strong> the business can pay someone else to own much of that web responsibility.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Cost is more than the subscription price.</h2>
  <p>
    A builder may appear cheaper because the monthly software fee is easy to
    compare with a professional website project. The missing number is the
    owner's time.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you spend evenings learning layout, mobile breakpoints, SEO settings,
    domains, Search Console, analytics, and troubleshooting, the software may be
    inexpensive while the total business cost is not.
  </p>

  <div class="guide-time-cost">
    <strong>A simple opportunity-cost example</strong>
    <p>
      Imagine a task would take you four hours to learn and complete, while a
      professional would charge $100 to handle it. Doing it yourself saves the
      $100 cash expense, but it also consumes four hours.
    </p>
    <p class="guide-example">
      If those four hours could have been used for client work, sales,
      production, leadership, or another activity worth more than $100 to the
      business, outsourcing may be the less expensive choice overall. If your
      time is available and you enjoy learning the system, DIY may still make
      perfect sense.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Who is a builder usually a good fit for?</h2>
  <p>
    A builder can make sense when budget is the primary constraint, the site is
    simple, the owner enjoys hands-on website work, and they are comfortable
    being responsible for ongoing changes and setup decisions.
  </p>

  <h2>Who benefits from a managed custom site?</h2>
  <p>
    A managed custom approach can make more sense when the owner wants a site
    tailored to the business, does not want to become the internal web person,
    values technical control, or wants someone to continue interpreting search
    and analytics after launch.
  </p>

